From 0d6bf27e1a85ebe44fc52bd4689f0e7faf04e284 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 21:59:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Check published update checksums before offering a
 download

Nightlink opens update downloads in the browser (desktop.cjs
updates:open uses shell.openExternal), so it cannot hash the file the
user downloads. It now confirms what GitHub publishes instead:

- Read SHA256SUMS.txt from the same release, accepting only the exact
  release download URL, following a redirect only to GitHub release
  storage over HTTPS, with a 16 KB cap and 10 s timeout. Malformed,
  duplicate, path-like or empty checksum files are rejected.
- Report the expected SHA-256 only when the asset digest and
  SHA256SUMS.txt agree. Disagreement is "mismatch"; one or both
  missing is "incomplete"/"missing"; a failed checksum fetch is
  "unavailable".
- Settings shows the expected SHA-256, copy buttons and the PowerShell
  Get-FileHash command. On mismatch the download button is hidden, the
  banner warns, and updates:open falls back to the release page.

The updates browser test now opens Settings > Data & updates before
checking the panel; it predated the categorised Settings layout.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
